<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Senguttuvan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chenguttuvan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Senguttuvan was a Chera king of the Sangam age in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, praised in the fifth decade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patirruppattu">Patirruppattu</a> for his campaigns. In the epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silappatikaram">Silappatikaram</a> he leads an expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> to bring a stone for an image of Kannagi, the heroine of the poem, establishing the cult of the goddess Pattini. His dates are uncertain, but he is usually placed around the 2nd century CE.</p></div>
